DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
nd
- a network disk, served by the network card
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
nd
device reads and writes 512 byte blocks that live at the other end of
the network, in the same card that carries the network's byte channel.
It is a block device and not a file system of its own: it has one node,
.bd
/dev/nd ,
and hands the buffer cache whole blocks the way any disk here does.
There is one of them in a machine.

Three registers are its whole interface.  0x60 reads the status: a bit
saying an operation has finished, and a bit saying it failed.  0x61
writes a command.  0x63 reads and writes a byte of the card's data area.
Two of the commands point that area at something - one at the block
number, one at the block's data - and two of them are the operations
themselves, fetch the block and store the block.  The block number is
four bytes, most significant first.

A request is therefore: point the card at the block number and write it,
then point it at the data and either read the block out of the data
register or write the block into it.  Every byte of the number and every
byte of the block moves through the one data port, which is to say the
cpu moves them - there is no dma controller on the card - and the 512
bytes are read or written in a tight loop.

There is no geometry.  A network disk is a flat run of blocks numbered
from zero, with no cylinders, heads or sectors to be told about, so
there is no label to read and none is asked for, and the device has no
slices: every block of it is reached through the one node.  There is no
raw command block either; a control call is refused with ENOTTY, since
formatting, seeking and geometry all mean nothing to a device with no
moving parts.

The card has one thing to say on its own, and that is that an operation
has finished.  It raises the interrupt the network card is on, and the
handler answers it by resuming the phase the request was waiting in -
the block number written, the data moving, the status read.  The line is
the driver's to claim, and it is claimed when the device is opened,
rather than at boot.

The card serves one request at a time.  A request that arrives while one
is in flight is queued behind it and started when the first finishes, so
the drive's queue is the only order the card has.

A request that is still in flight a second later is failed with ENXIO
rather than left waiting for ever.  The driver keeps a tick for it, and
that is what turns a machine whose card is not answering - or one whose
interrupt has not been claimed yet - into a request that fails instead
of a process that sleeps for ever.
.sp 2
